Package: gettoken
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-contract,
         gettoken-entitlements,
         gettoken-token-service
Description: token broker for AI agents
 An agent that holds a long-lived credential holds whatever authority that
 credential carries, and an agent can be talked into things by whatever it
 reads. gettoken hands an agent a token narrow enough for the job in front of
 it and nothing more, drawn from a super-token a human already holds.
 .
 This package contains the entry point the agent invokes and the privileged
 half that builds the request on its behalf. The agent never holds the
 super-token: it says what it wants, and gets back a token for that job.

Package: gettoken-token-service
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-contract
Description: token broker for AI agents - exchange dispatcher
 token-service authenticates a request, resolves what it asks for, and hands
 the capability to the exchanger registered for the service named by the
 capability's first segment.
 .
 It ships no exchanger. Each integrated tool installs its own into
 /usr/lib/gettoken/exchangers, so adding the hundredth tool costs what the
 first one did.

Package: gettoken-entitlements
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: token broker for AI agents - capability inventory
 entitlements answers what an agent has to work with. It is what
 "gettoken --list" shows, and it is also the control: a request for a
 capability it does not list is refused.

Package: gettoken-secret-manager
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken-contract
Description: token broker for AI agents - super-token store
 The store holds the super-tokens a human placed on the privileged side,
 keyed by who holds them and which service they are for, and keeps every
 version that was put in.
 .
 The store is /var/lib/gettoken/secrets, and no account but the privileged one
 can read it. Nothing an agent runs reaches it; only an exchanger does, and
 only for as long as it takes to trade a super-token for a narrow one. Purging
 this package takes the store with it.

Package: gettoken-contract
Architecture: any
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         ${shlibs:Depends}
Description: token broker for AI agents - wire contracts
 Every hop between components carries a document, and every document is
 checked against the contract that governs it. A schema no code path consults
 describes a document rather than governing it.
 .
 This package holds the contracts and the two programs that carry a document
 through one: parse reads a document and hands back its fields, format takes
 fields and writes the document. The contracts are installed as they are
 written and read that way: there is no bundling step between what is committed
 and what governs a document.

Package: integration-test-tool
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gettoken,
         gettoken-secret-manager,
         gettoken-contract
Description: token broker for AI agents - tool that verifies the chain
 The tool the verification suite integrates, standing in for a real one so the
 chain can be exercised without reaching a service. It runs on the token that
 integrationtest/ci/run downgrades to and refuses the super-token that token
 was traded for, so a run that succeeds is proof that a downgrade happened.
 .
 It is also the worked example of an integration: it depends on gettoken and
 carries one exchanger, which knows one service and nothing about any other.
 Installing it is what brings the chain onto a machine. A tool someone else
 already packages is integrated by a second package alongside theirs, because
 that packaging belongs to its own maintainer; this tool has no other, so it is
 one package.
